{"description":"<p><i>Beyond Good and Evil</i>, one of Nietzsche’s four “late period” works, is a philosophical treatise organized into nine parts and 296 short individual sections. In it he explores the concept of morality as taken for granted by contemporary philosophers, and whether “good” and “evil” should be considered just two sides of the same coin.</p>","chapters":[{"title":"Preface","words":713},{"title":"1 – 6","part":"I: Prejudices of Philosophers","words":1775},{"title":"7 – 11","part":"I: Prejudices of Philosophers","words":1729},{"title":"12 – 17","part":"I: Prejudices of Philosophers","words":1618},{"title":"18 – 21","part":"I: Prejudices of Philosophers","words":1743},{"title":"22 – 23","part":"I: Prejudices of Philosophers","words":719},{"title":"24 – 27","part":"II: The Free Spirit","words":1506},{"title":"28 – 32","part":"II: The Free Spirit","words":1827},{"title":"33 – 39","part":"II: The Free Spirit","words":1735},{"title":"40 – 44","part":"II: The Free Spirit","words":1739},{"title":"45 – 48","part":"III: The Religious Mood","words":1672},{"title":"49 – 58","part":"III: The Religious Mood","words":2176},{"title":"59 – 62","part":"III: The Religious Mood","words":1766},{"title":"Chapter 63","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":18},{"title":"Chapter 64","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":22},{"title":"Chapter 65","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":22},{"title":"65A","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":13},{"title":"66 – 73","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":153},{"title":"73A","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":14},{"title":"74 – 144","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":1572},{"title":"145 – 185","part":"IV: Apophthegms and Interludes","words":774},{"title":"186 – 188","part":"V: The Natural History of Morals","words":1600},{"title":"189 – 194","part":"V: The Natural History of Morals","words":2025},{"title":"195 – 201","part":"V: The Natural History of Moral